When zapped, asks for a direction. uses beam effect (like cancellation
or force bolt). No effect on most monsters, but zapped items (or all
applicable items in your inventory, if you zap yourself) May turn to
gold depending on what they were before. Golden weapons and armors do
not work as well as regular ones in combat (probably significantly so)
and weigh more, but sell for much more money. Silver weapons, wood
weapons, leather armor, cloth armor, crystal plate, and dragon scales/
scale mails are not affected. All rings (other than meat rings) turn
to gold rings (and gain the magic ability of whatever gold rings are,
keeping their BCU status and charge, if applicable). All non-wooden
wands turn to gold wands (which is another wand type added to
accommodate this new wand. The changed wands gain the magic ability of
whatever gold wands are). Zapped gems do nothing, but you can zap
statues and boulders (as if you'd be able to carry them after doing
so) as well as rocks and flint stones (very heavy, but doable). A gold
lodestone is obscenely heavy (we're talking upwards of 1000 units of
weight), however, shopkeepers will buy it off you for a descent price
(both eliminating your weight problem and netting you a nice profit).
Luckstones and touchstones turn in to the same thing as flints, so
that's not a good idea. Much like polymorph, this never works on
artifacts, and if you're piling too many things in one zap,  may
create a gold golem. also turns all zapped golems into gold golems.
Lastly, if you have any gold items in your inventory and are hit by a
leprechaun, he will steal as many gold items as he can carry instead
of your zorkmids (wielding an item cannot prevent this, but wearing it
can)

Of course, the inherit problem here is the balance issues. It would
effectively allow the player to buy out any shop he came across with
relative ease, plus all those zorkmids make a nice score booster; not
particularly fair to people who don't want to scum for things like
that. The other issue is: what if wands of wishing or death get
assigned to gold? Obvious balance issue there. Perhaps make it so that
those two wands are never assigned gold? Either way, probably not
viable, but fun to think about.


